`@Transactional` works via a Spring AOP proxy. The proxy consults the `PlatformTransactionManager`, opens/commits/rolls back a transaction around the method, and uses the `Propagation` attribute to decide whether to join an existing transaction or start a new one.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional                                         // PROPAGATION_REQUIRED (default)
    public void placeOrder(Order o) {
        orderRepo.save(o);
        auditService.record(o);                             // joins same transaction
        notificationService.notify(o);                      // see below
    }
}

@Service
class NotificationService {

    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)  // suspends outer, starts new
    public void notify(Order o) {
        notificationRepo.save(...);                         // commits independently of outer
    }
}

// --- The classic gotcha: self-invocation bypasses the proxy ---
@Service
public class BadService {
    @Transactional public void outer()  { this.inner(); }   // this = raw bean, NOT proxy
    @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
    public void inner() { /* runs in OUTER's tx, not a new one — proxy skipped! */ }
}

// --- Rollback rules: only RuntimeException triggers rollback by default ---
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)              // opt in to checked exceptions
public void transferFunds() throws IOException { ... }
```

| Propagation | Existing tx | No existing tx |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `REQUIRED` (default) | join | create new |
| `REQUIRES_NEW` | suspend outer, create new | create new |
| `NESTED` | savepoint inside outer | create new |
| `SUPPORTS` | join | run without tx |
| `MANDATORY` | join | throw |
| `NEVER` | throw | run without tx |

- The proxy exists on the **public** method of a Spring-managed **bean**. Calling from inside the same class (`this.method()`) bypasses it — extract to a separate bean or use `AopContext.currentProxy()` + `@EnableAspectJAutoProxy(exposeProxy=true)`.
- `REQUIRES_NEW` commits to the DB **before** the outer transaction commits, so partial work is durable even if the outer rolls back — use it for audit logs and outbox writes.
- Checked exceptions do **not** roll back by default. Add `rollbackFor = Exception.class` or throw a `RuntimeException`.

**Optimistic locking with `@Version` + `@Retryable`:**

```java
@Entity
public class Account {
    @Id Long id;
    BigDecimal balance;
    @Version Long version;
}

@Service
public class TransferService {

    @Retryable(
        value = { ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException.class,
                  OptimisticLockException.class },
        maxAttempts = 4,
        backoff = @Backoff(delay = 50, multiplier = 2.0, random = true))
    @Transactional
    public void transfer(Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        Account from = accountRepo.findById(fromId).orElseThrow();
        Account to   = accountRepo.findById(toId).orElseThrow();
        from.setBalance(from.getBalance().subtract(amount));
        to.setBalance(to.getBalance().add(amount));
    }

    @Recover
    public void transferFailed(OptimisticLockException e, Long fromId, Long toId, BigDecimal amount) {
        metrics.increment("transfer.optimistic_lock_exhausted");
        throw new TransferFailedException("Too much contention; try again later", e);
    }
}
```

Enable with `@EnableRetry`. `random = true` adds jitter so a thundering herd does not collide again on retry.

**Pessimistic locking with `@Lock` + lock timeout:**

```java
public interface SeatRepository extends JpaRepository<Seat, Long> {
    @Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
    @QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "jakarta.persistence.lock.timeout", value = "3000"))
    Optional<Seat> findByIdAndEventId(Long seatId, Long eventId);
}

@Service
public class SeatBookingService {
    @Transactional
    public Booking reserve(Long eventId, Long seatId, String userId) {
        Seat seat = seatRepo.findByIdAndEventId(seatId, eventId).orElseThrow();
        if (seat.isReserved()) throw new SeatAlreadyReservedException(seatId);
        seat.reserve(userId);
        return new Booking(eventId, seatId, userId);
    }
}
```

`lock.timeout = 3000` prevents waiting forever on a stuck lock. On timeout Hibernate throws `LockTimeoutException`.

**Hybrid pattern — optimistic by default, escalate to pessimistic on repeated failure:**

```java
@Service
public class InventoryService {
    @Retryable(value = OptimisticLockException.class, maxAttempts = 3)
    @Transactional
    public void decrementOptimistic(Long sku, int qty) {
        Inventory i = inventoryRepo.findBySku(sku).orElseThrow();
        i.decrement(qty);
    }

    @Recover
    public void fallbackToPessimistic(OptimisticLockException e, Long sku, int qty) {
        inventoryRepo.decrementPessimistic(sku, qty);
    }
}
```

Serves the common case without locking and only serializes on the hot SKU.
